<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
    Livestock price scale. The economic difficulty multiplies every fill type's price (EASY x3,
    NORMAL x1.8, HARD x1), but the mod's live-animal prices never read it, so on HARD the animals,
    meat and milk were calibrated for one scale and the map sells at another. This file scales the
    whole livestock economy together, on top of the difficulty:

    scale      Multiplies
               - every animal's live price (Animal:getSellPrice, which is also what the dealer's
                 buy price is made from with its markup) and the fixed sales for overcrowding and
                 disposal;
               - the dealer's semen straws and what your own dewars sell for;
               - the base price (pricePerLiter, and the price history the graphs start from) of
                 every fill type listed below, after the map's and other mods' fill types are loaded,
                 so every selling point, great demand and the annual mean the live prices follow
                 move with it.
    feeScale   Multiplies the contract slaughterhouse's fee (the map's fee per animal and the mod's
               cutting fee per kg, xml/carcassYield.xml) and the fees the live-price floor and
               market factor price in. Default: scale, so the calibration in carcassYield.xml
               (slaughtering by contract +12% over the year, never over +25%) holds at any scale.

    <basePrice fillType="..." price="..."/> sets a fill type's base price per litre (kg for meat) before
    the scale, in place of the map's; its seasonal factors stay the map's. The meat prices here are the
    ones the live-price curves are calibrated at (docs RLRM_牛肉链收益校准.md, RLRM_猪羊禽兔屠宰与售价校准.md):
    the annual mean at the tavern and farm shop (price scale 1) is the calibration price, so slaughtering
    by contract earns about 12% over the year there, and about 25% at the supermarket (1.1).
    Hof Bergmann ships beef at 1.8, pork 1.5, lamb 2.3, poultry 1.3, goose 2.1, rabbit 1.8.

    The list holds the products animals give and the goods made only from them, so that processing
    them still pays: a dairy's cheese must stay worth more than its milk. Goods also made from crops
    (fabric from cotton as well as wool, cakes) are left out; a fill type the map lacks is skipped.
    Transport fees, feed, bedding and other costs are not scaled.

    scale 1 turns the file off. To change it without editing the mod, copy this file to
    modSettings/FS25_RealisticLivestockRM/livestockPrices.xml; that copy then replaces this file
    entirely. In multiplayer the server's file applies and every client must have the same one.
-->
<livestockPrices scale="3">
    <basePrice fillType="BEEFMEAT"      price="9.43"/>
    <basePrice fillType="PORKMEAT"      price="4.30"/>
    <basePrice fillType="SHEEPGOATMEAT" price="14.94"/>
    <basePrice fillType="POULTRYMEAT"   price="9.05"/>
    <basePrice fillType="GOOSEMEAT"     price="10.20"/>
    <basePrice fillType="RABBITMEAT"    price="10.34"/>

    <!-- meat -->
    <fillTypes names="BEEFMEAT PORKMEAT SHEEPGOATMEAT POULTRYMEAT GOOSEMEAT RABBITMEAT HORSEMEAT"/>
    <!-- milk and dairy -->
    <fillTypes names="MILK GOATMILK BUFFALOMILK MILK_BOTTLED MILK_BOTTLED_SOLO GOATMILK_BOTTLED BUFFALOMILK_BOTTLED CREAM LOWFATMILK BUTTER CHEESE GOATCHEESE BUFFALOMOZZARELLA"/>
    <!-- eggs, wool, feathers, breeding -->
    <fillTypes names="EGG EGG_HB QUAILEGG CHICKS SHEEPWOOL_SHEARED SHEEPWOOL_CLEANEDWET SHEEPWOOL_CLEANEDDRY SHEEPWOOL_CLEANED GOOSEFEATHERS BULLSPERM"/>
</livestockPrices>
